========================================================================
5. A common mistake: letting Spark guess the column types
========================================================================
first CSV lines: ['zip_code,customer,amount_due', '95050,Chen,120.00', '10001,Okafor,45.10']

With inferSchema=True:
root
 |-- zip_code: integer (nullable = true)
 |-- customer: string (nullable = true)
 |-- amount_due: double (nullable = true)

+--------+---------+----------+
|zip_code| customer|amount_due|
+--------+---------+----------+
|    2138|   Murphy|    310.25|
|   10001|Bernstein|     19.99|
|   10001|   Okafor|      45.1|
+--------+---------+----------+
only showing top 3 rows
'02138' became 2138: a 4-digit ZIP code the post office can't deliver.

Without inferSchema (all strings), 'largest amount first':
+--------+---------+----------+
|zip_code| customer|amount_due|
+--------+---------+----------+
|   98101| Andersen|     99.00|
|   98101|   Larsen|      9.50|
|   60601| Kowalski|     88.00|
|   73301|Hernandez|     75.00|
|   95050|  Alvarez|     64.75|
+--------+---------+----------+
only showing top 5 rows
The real largest amount, '310.25', is not even in the top 5.

With an explicit schema:
+--------+---------+----------+
|zip_code| customer|amount_due|
+--------+---------+----------+
|   02138|   Murphy|    310.25|
|   10001|Bernstein|     19.99|
|   10001|   Okafor|      45.1|
+--------+---------+----------+
only showing top 3 rows
largest amounts correct? True
